0 \cdot 1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 = 0 \Rightarrow \text{Product is 0}
$$

This is equal to: $$
\frac{(n+5)!}{(n-1)!}
$$

So we want: $$
\frac{(n+5)!}{(n-1)!} \text{ is a perfect square}
$$

It is known that the only **positive integer** $ n $ for which the product of 6 consecutive integers is a perfect square is: $$
\boxed{n = 1}
$$

